Via logistic regression with cancer as the outcome, we can see that family history is related to biopsy outcome with odds ratio 2.32 (95% CI: 1.44, 3.71; p<0.001).
The decision curve analysis can help us address the clinical utility of using family history to predict biopsy outcome.





#### {.tabset .tabset-pills}

##### R {.active}

::: {.cell class='R'}

```{.r .cell-code}
dca(cancer ~ famhistory, data = df_cancer_dx) %>%
  plot()
```
:::

##### Stata 

::: {.cell class='Stata'}

```{.stata .cell-code}
* Run the decision curve: family history is coded as 0 or 1, i.e. a probability
* so no need to specify the “probability” option
dca cancer famhistory
```
:::

##### SAS 

::: {.cell class='SAS'}

```{.sas .cell-code}
* Run the decision curve: family history is coded as 0 or 1, i.e. a probability, so no need to specify the “probability” option;
%DCA(data = data_cancer, outcome = cancer, predictors = famhistory, graph = yes);
```
:::

##### Python 

::: {.cell class='Python'}

```{.python .cell-code}
  dca_famhistory_df = \
        dca(
            data=df_cancer_dx,
            outcome='cancer',
            modelnames=['famhistory']
        )

    plot_graphs(
        plot_df=dca_famhistory_df,
        graph_type='net_benefit',
        y_limits=[-0.05, 0.2]
    )
```
:::

